now adari was a firsthand account of  jewish life in hiding during the second  world war now three-quarters of a  century after the death of anne frank a  new investigation has identified a  suspect who may have betrayed her family  to the nazis while anne frank and her  family spent 761 days over two years  hidden behind a bookcase in a house in  amsterdam they were captured on the  morning of the 4th of august 1944  probably after the location was handed  to the nazis by arnold van der beau a  jewish man who it's thought gave up the  franks to save his own loved ones they  were sent to auschwitz a month later and  her sisters were then moved to  bergen-belsen a concentration camp in  norman northern germany where they  contracted typhus and died in february  1945  and was just 15 years old  well joining me now is the retired fbi  special agent vince pankow who's  director of investigation for the anne  frank cold case um  if it's just tell us about this this new  research how were you able to find this  new link to arnold van der beau  well surprisingly the link was there all  along it was just like in many cold  cases an overlooked clue detail that we  found  in the report of the  1963-64 investigator  and and why was it not spotted that this  link could be more significant  well in in the report itself the  investigator said that he looked into  this allegation of a note that otto  frank told him about  and that  through assimilated intelligence he  realized that  the  character of arnold vandenberg was  beyond reproach and he just dropped it  but how do you believe now that that he  was potentially the man who gave up the  location of where they were all hiding  well in the report otto frank  informed the detective that shortly  after liberation he received a note that  identified arnold vandenberg as the  betrayer  when we did our research we found out  arnold vandenberg was in the jewish  council we knew the jewish council was  abolished in september of 43 and so we  figured the the substance of the note  couldn't be true but of course we found  out that arnold vandenberg was in hiding  in plain sight he had a series of  purchased protections  from  deportation and when those expired he  had lists of addresses not names but  addresses  where jews were thought to be in hiding  which he passed to keep his safety and  his freedom  and do you believe that otto frank and  frank's father knew  who it was  well of course otto was the one who  received the note and i think that he  knew the name he even took steps in 1945  by confronting one of the dutch  detectives who participated in the raid  with the name of arnold vandenberg  so  yes now whether or not at that point he  actually believes beyond reasonable  doubt that that vandenberg was  responsible we're not sure that he did  take steps to investigate him though  and of course the family were so very  close weren't they they were so very  close to freedom at that time  yeah that i just was talking to a  gentleman who experienced world war ii  uh yesterday and said that the picture  of otto frank  watching the map  of normandy and in that part of europe  where they were almost liberated by the  allies would have been just such a tease  such a roller coaster of emotion  indeed why is it important now  to to to find out who this person was  and why they did it do you think  well i think we owe it to the memory of  the victims of the annex of the  survivors of the holocaust and the  victims of the holocaust to let them  know that you know there we haven't  forgot about them and to take a bigger  message from this that  this type of action could still happen  today once we start to tear at the  fabric of society eventually that  garment starts to fall apart  and it's important for people to realize  that uh you know even a cultured a  modernized country like germany in the  1930s fell prey to this and almost  dominated the world  we talked a little bit about our arnold  van der berg and and his motivation for  doing this your  is your supposition that he he didn't do  it to necessarily betray the family but  he did it because he felt it was  the one thing he could do to save his  own  yes there's no direct evidence that  indicates that arnold vandenberg even  knew the franks but he had lists of  addresses  impersonal lists of addresses where jews  were thought to be in hiding that he  transmitted to the sd  um we tell people to make sure not to  make the mistake of judging arnold  vandenberg or any of the other jews that  were forced into collaboration the  people to blame here are the nazi  occupiers and without for that  we wouldn't be even talking about this  today  and it does now complete something of a  story doesn't it of wartime amsterdam  we are still learning all the time  about things that happened during this  era which is astonishing really isn't it  that there's still so much to be  uncovered  when you think about how much time has  passed since world war ii which of  course my father was a veteran of the  war in europe  and that there is still new information  coming out documents like the one that  we found  that was in the papers of the  detective who had passed away years ago  it's astonishing there's still stories  out there to be told  astonishing and and also very gloomy uh  vince pankow really interesting to talk  to you very very much thanks a lot for  joining us today  thank you
 

